When I type "Hey!" and hit send, a lot happens in a second.

Application layer: This is just the WhatsApp app I'm using. I type my message and choose who to send it to. The app gets the message ready with the text, the person's name and the time.

Presentation layer: Before the message leaves my phone, it gets scrambled (encrypted) so nobody in between can read it. Only my friend's phone has the key to unscramble it. The message is also converted into a format computers can handle easily.

Session layer: My phone opens a connection with WhatsApp's server and keeps it going while we chat. This layer makes sure we stay connected, and if my network drops for a moment, it helps pick things back up.

Transport layer: The message is broken into small pieces and each piece is numbered. That way, if one gets lost on the way, it can be sent again, and everything can be put back in the right order at the end.

Network layer: Each piece gets an address label (IP address) showing where it came from and where it's going. Routers read these labels and pass the pieces along the best route across the internet.

Data link layer: The pieces are passed from one device to the next, like from my phone to my Wi-Fi router. This layer also checks for errors so damaged data doesn't get through.

Physical layer: Finally, everything is turned into actual signals, like radio waves for Wi-Fi or mobile data, or light through fiber cables. This is the real "travelling" part.

On the receiving side: My friend's phone does all of this in reverse. The signals become data, the pieces are put back in order, the message is unscrambled, and WhatsApp shows "Hey!" on their screen.
